23:45:57 | INFO | 🤖 Target: You’re absolutely right—for anything built in the last 60+ years, it’s all negative-ground. I brought up the vintage examples only to show that the choice of *which* terminal to ground is completely independent of the direction of electron flow. But since that’s a pointless distraction for modern cars, let me just focus on the core of your question. The key thing is that in a car, **the chassis is just a big, shared return wire**. It doesn’t matter whether you call the moving charges “conventional current” or “electrons”—the chassis simply provides a low-resistance path back to the battery. From an electron-flow perspective, the negative-ground setup is actually perfectly logical: - Electrons are pushed out of the battery’s **negative** terminal. - They travel through the **chassis** (which is connected to that negative terminal) to reach the negative side of every light, motor, and radio. - Then they flow through the device, doing work, and finally return to the battery’s **positive** terminal. So the chassis literally acts as the **source of electrons** for every circuit. That’s completely consistent with the idea that electrons leave the negative post. If you instead used a positive-ground system (like the old cars you rightly ignore), the chassis would be the *destination* for electrons—also works, but it’s the opposite arrangement. The real reason the *negative* terminal got chosen as the universal standard for modern cars has nothing to do with electron flow theory and everything to do with practical engineering: - **Less corrosion**: A negatively charged chassis resists rusting (it becomes the cathode instead of the anode). - **Better spark plug performance**: Firing the center electrode negative gives a more reliable spark. - **Solid-state electronics**: When alternators and diodes became standard, a negative-ground system was simpler to design. So in the end, the electron flow direction and the ground polarity are two separate things. The chassis is just a conductor; it could be connected to either battery terminal. Modern cars happen to use the negative terminal for all those practical reasons, and that makes the chassis the electron source—perfectly aligned with the physics you already understand.
